\section{Covariates}{

With a formula such as \code{score ~ age + gender}, the outcome and every
column of the document vectors are first regressed on the formula's fixed
effects by least squares, and PLS runs on the residuals. \code{r2} is then the
residualized outcome's, and the residuals the fit reports have the
covariates taken out. The covariates' own coefficients are not reported:
use \code{\link[=fit_regression]{fit_regression()}} for them, and for random effects, which \code{fit_pls()}
does not take.
}

\section{Several gradients}{

With \code{ssd_gradients = N > 1}, the result also holds \code{N} gradients,
\code{units[["dim-1"]]} ... \code{units[["dim-N"]]}, from the weight vectors of an
N-component PLS fit on the same data. With \code{rotate = "words"} they are
varimax-rotated towards the projection of the 50,000 most frequent words
of the pack, ordered by the correlation of their scores with the outcome,
and signed so that it is positive. \code{rotate = NULL} keeps them in PLS
order. The standard fit does not change.
}

